  **D11. Banding (section 4).** Each band: seeds only in core [band_lo, band_hi) bins, but `visited` is per-band, and extension is unlimited. What can go wrong:
  - Two bands can trace over the same peaks: band A extends into band B's core and consumes peaks that B would have used as seeds or members. Since visited is per-band, B doesn't see A's takings. Consequences: (a) duplicate traces: band A's trace T (seeded in A's core) and band B's trace T' (seeded in B's core at a peak A consumed) may cover the same underlying signal partially. Actually if A consumed B's peaks, B's seed at a taken peak still seeds (visited is local!) — B seeds at a peak that in the unbanded run would have been marked taken by A's valid trace. So B produces a trace that OpenMS/unbanded would not produce — a near-duplicate overlapping trace. Both survive → downstream correlation sees a duplicated fragment. OpenMS's halo scheme has the same class (0.1% measured). The duplication is bounded: a band can only seed in its own core, so duplicates arise only where a trace crosses a core boundary AND another valid seed exists within the crossed region. Unbounded in count? Each crossing trace can "shadow" multiple seeds in the neighbor core. It's bounded by the number of seeds near boundaries; measured ~0.1% for the halo variant.
  - Worse: ORDER effects. In the unbanded run, the most intense apex seeds first and takes peaks, suppressing later seeds. In the banded run, each band runs its own intensity order; a moderately intense seed in band B that would have been suppressed in the unbanded run (because band A's stronger trace took its peaks) now fires and creates a trace with STOLEN-BUT-UNAWARE membership: its trace may terminate early or accept different peaks? No — it accepts peaks regardless of A's visited marks, so B's trace actually gets the same peaks it would have if A never existed. So banding can only ADD traces (extra traces that would have been suppressed), never remove... wait, can it remove? Within a band, everything is self-consistent: the band's run is exactly the unbanded run restricted to seeds in the core, with extension over all peaks. The unbanded run = single pass with global visited. A trace T seeded in band A in the banded run is identical to the unbanded trace IF at the time T seeds in the unbanded run, the same peaks are unvisited. But in the unbanded run, a stronger trace from band B's core (seeding earlier globally) may have consumed some of T's peaks first, changing T's membership/validity — in the banded run T still gets them. So banding changes SET both ways: can keep traces the unbanded run would have altered/dropped, and creates duplicates near boundaries. Bounded by boundary-adjacent activity; the global intensity ordering is partially lost (only within-band ordering preserved). Also EPD splitting afterwards can split the duplicates differently.
